=== 3. DOES THE AXIS HAVE POWER? ===
Over the 40-1700 Hz fit band (16 bands), vs the
4-19 dB drvspr that pre-clipper elements actually deliver:

  would have DETECTED a pre-clipper carrier (thr < 4 dB): 15 bands [40, 50, 64, 80, 101, 127, 160, 202, 254, 403, 508, 640, 806, 1016, 1613]
  would NOT have detected it (thr >= 4 dB):            0 bands []
  BLIND at every span tested (no threshold at all):           1 bands [320]

=> the drive axis HAS the power to detect a pre-clipper carrier.
   ⚠ Wide theta intervals do NOT imply no power: the residual test constrains the
   MAGNITUDE ladder's shape, and mu_spr spans 4-25 dB, so a drive-dependent ramp
   cannot be absorbed even where theta is free. My going-in hypothesis was wrong.


=== 4. SO WHAT DOES IT ACTUALLY SAY ABOUT THE SHIPPED MODEL? ===
Having established the axis CAN see drive-dependence, invert each band's power curve
to ask: how much drive-dependence would produce the residual we ACTUALLY observe?

⚠ This attributes the WHOLE residual to drive-dependence, so it is an UPPER BOUND on
  the drive-dependent part (capture noise, band leakage and errors in mu_d's own shape
  all land here too). It cannot prove the carrier is pre-clipper. What it CAN do is
  test the claim that the residual is small enough to call the defect drive-INDEPENDENT.

